\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rates (CAR) on safety benchmarks, yet we show these metrics are contaminated by action collapse, where near-zero motor output produces zero collisions through inaction rather than avoidance. We introduce displacement-filtered CAR (dfCAR), which excludes sub-threshold episodes, revealing that over 99\% of safe episodes in Pi0-family models are action-collapse artifacts. We further show that end-effector-only Signed Distance Field (SDF) supervision fails to deliver collision avoidance: ground-truth oracle refinement reduces CAR below unrefined baselines by redistributing collisions to unmonitored arm links, and SDF training supervision shows no statistically significant benefit beyond collision-free demonstrations alone. These findings suggest that collision avoidance primarily arises from demonstration data rather than EE-only geometric supervision.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We tested whether SDF supervision and collision-aware evaluation deliver reliable geometric safety for VLAs. Action collapse renders reported CAR illusory; dfCAR corrects this using only EE position logging (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}). EE-only oracle SDF refinement is counterproductive, reducing CAR by 5--10\,pp as unmonitored links rotate through obstacles (\S\ref{sec:ee_only}), and collision-free demonstrations account for nearly all avoidance gains with no statistically significant SDF benefit (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). More broadly, dfCAR provides a lightweight diagnostic that any VLA pipeline can adopt to screen for action-collapse contamination, and point-supervision of a single link is insufficient for articulated robots; genuine geometric safety will require full-body collision representations and cross-architecture validation.

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

All experiments use MuJoCo simulation (SafeLIBERO, two VLA families, four pick-and-place tasks); action collapse is structurally general but untested on other benchmarks or real hardware. dfCAR assumes bimodal displacement separation (Figure~\ref{fig:displacement}), which may require task-specific calibration when less pronounced. The EE-only SDF null result is specific to auxiliary loss integration and should not be extrapolated to constrained optimization or runtime filtering (Section~\ref{sec:related}). Oracle refinement uses a single seed per variant ($n{=}1$, 160 episodes). The training ablation ($n{=}3$ seeds; MDE ${\approx}$ 5.6\,pp at 80\% power) cannot rule out a small SDF effect (2--5\,pp) without larger samples.

